window.docContents[74].resolve({"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--Tangential-coordinate-changes":{"contents":"For two charts, define the overlap parameter domainThe tangential coordinate transition is\n\nOpenness makes the overlap measurable and regularity supplies a derivative.\nTogether with injectivity, these are the hypotheses of the same-dimensional\nchange-of-variables theorem applied to .\n\nThe overlap  is open, and\n is globally .\n\nThe overlap is the intersection of the open parameter domain with the\npreimage of the open source of chart  under the continuous map .\nThe transition is a composition of , chart , and the\ncontinuous linear flat projection, all globally .\n\nThe transition is designed so that both charts parametrize the same physical\npoint. Verifying this also shows that  lands in the parameter\ndomain of chart , because a frontier point has zero height in every chart\ncontaining it.\n\nFor ,and\n\nThe point  lies on the physical frontier. Its image under chart\n therefore has last coordinate zero. Re-embedding its tangential\nprojection recovers the full flattened point. The chart right-inverse\nidentity then gives the parametrization equality.\n\nSince  is injective on , the identity\n determines  uniquely, and the\ntransition in the opposite direction must undo it. Injectivity of the\ntransition and the exact identification of its image are then formal\nconsequences.\n\nThe transition sends  into , and the\nreverse transition is its inverse there:Consequently,  is injective on  and\n\nBoth  and  parametrize the same physical\nboundary point through . Injectivity of  on its parameter\ndomain proves the inverse identity. That identity gives injectivity.\nApplying the same result with  and  exchanged gives surjectivity onto\nthe reverse overlap.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Boundary Calculus and C¹ Boundary Geometry","header":"3.3.2.8. Tangential coordinate changes","id":"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Boundary-Calculus-and-C___-Boundary-Geometry--Tangential-coordinate-changes"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--Energy-and-Leray___Hopf-solutions":{"contents":"For a differentiable velocity field, the convective power is transport of\nthe specific kinetic energy :Where pressure is also differentiable, pressure work satisfiesUnder incompressibility, the pressure contribution is a spatial divergence.\nThe kinetic-energy flux obeysThus incompressibility also turns the convective power into\n.\n\nDifferentiate  componentwise and apply the product\nrule. Multiplication by  and summation over  gives the convective\nidentity. Applying the product rule to each  and\n gives the two flux identities; their compressibility\nterms vanish when .\n\nThe abstract time-regularity step behind this calculation is the\nLions–Magenes energy\nidentity. Under the Gelfand-triple hypotheses stated there, an \ntrajectory with weak time derivative in  has a continuous\n-valued representative and an exact squared-norm identity. Applying it to\nNavier–Stokes requires the equation to supply that dual-space time regularity.\n\nFor a smooth solution with zero forcing and enough spatial decay and\nintegrability to differentiate under the integral and integrate by parts,\nmultiply the Navier–Stokes equation by  and integrate over\n:\n\n\n\nThe pressure term vanishes by incompressibility:\n. The nonlinear term\nalso vanishes:\n.\nThese cancellations give the energy identity:\n\n\n\nKinetic energy decreases by the accumulated viscous dissipation.\n\nA pressure-free weak solution on  stores  membership of every\nvelocity slice and of every weak-gradient slice at positive times. The\ncorresponding Hilbert-space classes arethe classes of  and , extended by the\nzero class outside  and  respectively, together with the class\n of the prescribed initial velocity. The\nvelocity slice is also recorded as an element of  carrying the weak\ntopology, which is the topology in which Leray–Hopf solutions are\ncontinuous in time.\n\nFor a weak solution, the physical energy quantities are the squared Hilbert\nnorms of the corresponding  classes:If moreover  belongs to , that is, the\ngradient slice map is square integrable in time on , then the weak\ndissipation  is integrable on . These\nidentities let the energy inequality of a Leray–Hopf solution be read either\nas a statement about integrals of the representative or as a statement in the\nHilbert space .\n\nThe squared  norm is the  inner product of the class with\nitself, which Mathlib expresses as the integral of the pointwise inner\nproduct. The class agrees almost everywhere with the chosen slice, so the\nintegrands coincide almost everywhere with the kinetic-energy and\nsquared-gradient densities, up to the constant one half. For time\nintegrability, membership of the slice map in Bochner  makes its\nsquared norm integrable, and the pointwise identity transfers integrability\nto the dissipation.\n\nThe Leray–Hopf class uses both readings: its a priori bounds are\n and  membership of the slice maps,\nwhile its energy inequality is written with the integrals  and\n.\n\nThe Leray–Hopf definition adds to the pressure-free weak solution\n velocity,  weak gradient, continuity in the\nweak  topology, the prescribed  initial trace,\nforce-work integrability, and the energy inequality measured from the initial\ndatum. It uses a chosen representative: velocity slices and the initial trace\nare imposed at every time in  and the weak gradient at every time in\n, while the initial datum is required only to lie in .A Leray–Hopf solution with a pressure representative supplies the separate\npressure-retaining refinement and projects to this pressure-free class.For this representative, the energy inequality holds for every\n:The force-work term measures external energy input. At zero forcing, the\ninequality bounds kinetic energy plus accumulated weak-gradient dissipation\nby the initial kinetic energy. Equality would require the additional\nregularity needed to justify the smooth integrations by parts.\n\nThe linear weak-evolution chapter already supplies\nGalerkin weak compactness.\nFor Navier–Stokes, weak convergence alone does not identify the quadratic\nterm . On bounded subdomains,\nRellich–Kondrachov compactness\nand Aubin–Lions compactness are the\nmodel inputs for obtaining strong spacetime convergence. Once strong\n convergence is available,\nthe bilinear -to-\nlimit theorem identifies the corresponding quadratic product. A whole-space\nconstruction also needs localization and control at infinity. These are the\nadditional nonlinear compactness steps between the abstract parabolic\nmachinery and Leray's global weak existence theorem.\n\nThe product estimate shows exactly where strong convergence enters. Write\n\n\n\nOn a fixed spacetime region, Hölder's inequality gives\n\n\n\nStrong  convergence makes the first factor vanish and bounds the\nsecond. Weak convergence preserves the linear divergence constraint, but\ndoes not provide this vanishing factor for convection. This is the role of\ncompactness in an existence proof, beyond writing down the weak equation.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Stokes and Navier–Stokes Flow\u0009Stokes and Incompressible Navier–Stokes","header":"8.5.1.6. Energy and Leray–Hopf solutions","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--Energy-and-Leray___Hopf-solutions"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Classical-Heat-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--The-Heat-Operator-and-Classical-Problems--The-spacetime-operator":{"contents":"With coordinate zero reserved for time, the classical heat operator on\n isHeatEquation.heatOperator is an ordinary function on Euclidean spacetime.\nIt computes the time-coordinate partial derivative and the Laplacian of the\nfixed-time spatial slice. Like Mathlib's Fréchet derivative, it is defined\nfor every u, and the analytic theorems state the regularity they need.\n\nSuppose  and  are differentiable at  and their fixed-time\nspatial slices are  at . Then the heat operator preserves their\nsum at . It also commutes with multiplication by a real constant:The scalar identity holds without regularity assumptions on , using the\ntotal derivative operators. These two calculus lemmas supply the\nsuperposition results for the homogeneous, forced, and initial-value problems.\n\nExpand . For a sum, apply the pointwise sum rules to\nthe time derivative and to the Laplacian of the fixed-time spatial slices,\nthen collect terms. For a scalar multiple, pull the constant through both\noperators and distribute it across the difference.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Classical Heat Flow\u0009The Heat Operator and Classical Problems","header":"6.2.1.1. The spacetime operator","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Classical-Heat-Flow/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Classical-Heat-Flow--The-Heat-Operator-and-Classical-Problems--The-spacetime-operator"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Aubin___Lions-Compactness":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation","header":"6.3.1.10. Aubin–Lions Compactness","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Aubin___Lions-Compactness"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Semilinear-Existence-and-Stability/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Semilinear-Existence-and-Stability--Semilinear-Elliptic-Methods--Monotone-Iteration-Between-Barriers":{"contents":"Let the ambient ordered Hilbert space have closed order relation, let the\nlinear form be bounded and coercive, and let  be continuous.\nAssume  and that the semilinear Picard map\nis monotone and preserves .\nIf the iterates from the lower endpoint\nconverge, their limit is the least weak solution in the interval. If the\niterates from the upper endpoint converge, their limit is the greatest weak\nsolution there.Convergence of the iterates is a hypothesis, since order monotonicity gives\nno compactness or strong convergence in the ambient Hilbert space.\n\nContinuity turns each assumed iterative limit into a fixed point. The\norder-interval iteration theorem compares every fixed point with every lower\nor upper iterate, and closedness of the order passes those inequalities to\nthe limit. The fixed-point equation is then rewritten as the semilinear weak\nequation.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Semilinear Existence and Stability\u0009Semilinear Elliptic Methods","header":"9.5.1.4. Monotone Iteration Between Barriers","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Semilinear-Existence-and-Stability/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Semilinear-Existence-and-Stability--Semilinear-Elliptic-Methods--Monotone-Iteration-Between-Barriers"}});